  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/...\nIssued 216 AM MST SUN NOV 22 2026\nA potent upper level trough will then move onshore on \nWednesday, rapidly weakening by the time it reaches eastern \nOregon. Models disagree on extent of precipitation, but light \nvalley rain and high elevation mountain snow will likely come to\nsoutheast Oregon and high terrain in southwest Idaho by \nWednesday night. Snow levels look to rise to near 5500-6500 \nfeet, although snow cover and lingering cold pools in high \nterrain valleys will allow for pockets of lower snow levels. \nAnother upper level trough extending down along the western \nCanada/US coast from a Gulf of Alaska low will likely dampen \nthe amplitude of high pressure over the region on Thursday. This\nwill bring zonal flow over the region and advect moisture with \na warm front into the northern tier of the area. The greatest \nuncertainty with this system lies in primarily the magnitude \nand extent of the precipitation on Thursday. Cluster guidance \nfavors high pressure pushing the best precipitation chances \nnorthward on Friday, with the mountains in northeast Oregon and \ncentral Idaho seeing the highest precipitation chances. If this \nhigh pressure effectively keeps most of the moisture and zonal \nflow to the north, as modeled by the AI-GFS deterministic \nsolution, inversion concerns with low stratus and fog in the \nSnake River Valley could return by late week. This warmer air \nwould also elevate snow levels to near 6500-7500 ft MSL, \nespecially in the Snake River Valley and near the NV border. \n\nForecast guidance becomes increasingly messy by the weekend, with \nseveral solutions favoring active and unsettled weather with \nlingering precipitation. All model solutions depict some form of \nanother upper level trough or closed low by Sunday, with widely \nvarying storm tracks and impacts for the region. Current forecast \nfavors a 30-50% chance of precipitation for lower elevations, with \nbetter confidence for high terrain in Idaho with a 50-70% \nchance of measurable precipitation.",
